<!doctype html>
<html lang="<%= LANGUAGE %>">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title><%= deck.title %></title>
<!-- One slide, standing still. The same stylesheets the presentation uses
     and in the same order, so the picture on the card IS the slide -- what
     it does without is reveal.js, the highlighter and the player, because
     a list of slides must not start a dozen presentations to show itself.
     thumb.css takes over the little reveal.js would have done for the
     layout. -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= BASE %>/reveal/reset.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= BASE %>/reveal/reveal.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= BASE %>/reveal/theme/<%= deck.theme %>.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= BASE %>/reveal-plugin/highlight/monokai.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= BASE %>/code-styles.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= BASE %>/static/css/slides.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="<%= BASE %>/static/css/thumb.css">
</head>
<body class="is-thumb">
<!-- "center" is not a decision made here, it is reveal.js' own: it puts
     that class on this element whenever its `center` setting is on, which
     is how it ships and what this project leaves it at. It carries a rule
     of its own (min-height: 0 on every slide), and a slide that says
     min-height in the layout stylesheet -- bild-voll does -- comes out a
     different height without it. So it is written here rather than
     discovered as a puzzle later. -->
<div class="reveal center">
<%- background %>
<div class="slides">
<%- slide %>
</div>
</div>
<!-- The two that a still picture does want. slide-video.js puts the video's
     own still image where the player would be (a card has no room for a
     player and no business starting one), slide-fit.js steps the type down
     on a slide that holds more than fits -- which is exactly the slide one
     wants to spot in the list. -->
<script src="<%= BASE %>/static/js/slide-video.js"></script>
<script src="<%= BASE %>/static/js/slide-fit.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
